---
/*
 * Privacy notice.
 *
 * Nevada requires this. NRS 603A.340 obliges an operator of a website that
 * collects personal information from Nevada consumers to post a notice
 * covering: the categories collected, the categories of third parties it is
 * shared with, the process for a consumer to review and request changes to
 * their information, whether any third party may collect information about a
 * consumer's activity across different websites, and an effective date. A
 * Reno business whose own FAQ says it serves Reno, Sparks and Carson City is
 * squarely in scope.
 *
 * Everything below was written against what the site actually does, verified
 * in the built output rather than assumed:
 *
 *   - The consultation form is the ONLY place the site collects anything.
 *   - No analytics of any kind. No Google Analytics, no Meta pixel, nothing.
 *   - No cookies. The site never calls document.cookie, localStorage,
 *     sessionStorage or indexedDB — so there is no cookie banner because
 *     there is nothing to consent to.
 *   - Exactly three external origins are contacted on a normal page view:
 *     fonts.googleapis.com and fonts.gstatic.com for the typefaces, and
 *     api.web3forms.com only at the moment someone submits the form.
 *
 * If any of that changes — an analytics script, an embedded map, a booking
 * widget — this page has to change with it. That is the maintenance cost of
 * making specific claims instead of vague ones, and it is worth paying: a
 * notice that lists cookies the site does not set is not a safer notice, it
 * is an inaccurate one.
 *
 * All three open questions were closed by the client on 2026-08-29:
 *   - There is no separate incorporated entity. "Bell of the Ball" is the
 *     business, so the notice names it and nothing else. (The Instagram handle
 *     reading "belloftheballinc" is just a handle.) Rendered the way the rest
 *     of the site sets it — "Bell of the Ball" — so the notice matches the
 *     footer's copyright line and every page title.
 *   - Effective date is the launch date, hard-coded below. It must NOT be
 *     derived from `new Date()`: an effective date states when these terms
 *     took effect, so it changes only when the notice itself changes. A date
 *     that silently follows the clock would claim the current version has
 *     always been in force.
 *   - No mailing address. Not required by NRS 603A, and the client does not
 *     want one published. Email is the contact route throughout.
 *
 * NOT legal advice. This is a careful, accurate draft written from the site's
 * real behaviour; have the client's attorney read it before launch.
 */
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CTAButton from '../components/CTAButton.astro';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL } from '../data/site';

/*
 * Static by design — see the note above. Update this by hand, and only when
 * the notice's substance changes.
 */
const EFFECTIVE = 'August 29, 2026';
const LEGAL_NAME = 'Bell of the Ball';
---

<Layout
  title="Privacy | Bell of the Ball"
  description="What Bell of the Ball collects when you use this site, who it is shared with, and how to ask us to change or delete it."
>
  <section class="hero" style="min-height: 45vh;">
    <div class="hero-content">
      <span class="section-label reveal">YOUR INFORMATION</span>
      <h1
        class="reveal"
        style="font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 5rem); line-height: 1; margin: 1rem 0 2rem; color: var(--color-warm-ink);"
      >
        Privacy Notice
      </h1>
      <p class="hero-tagline reveal" style="max-width: 560px;">
        The short version: the consultation form is the only thing on this site that collects anything, we do
        not track you, and we have never sold anyone's information.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="about" style="padding-top: 0;">
    <div class="about-container legal">
      <p class="legal-meta reveal">
        <span class="label-text">Effective</span>
        <span>{EFFECTIVE}</span>
      </p>

      <div class="legal-body reveal">
        <h2>Who this is about</h2>
        <p>
          This notice covers <strong>{LEGAL_NAME}</strong> and this website. We are an event planning, design,
          officiating, notary and rental business based in Reno, Nevada, serving Reno, Sparks and Carson City.
        </p>

        <h2>What we collect, and when</h2>
        <p>
          The consultation form is the only part of this site that collects anything about you. If you fill it
          in, you give us:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Your name and email address.</strong> Both required — the email is how we reply.</li>
          <li><strong>Your phone number</strong>, if you choose to give it. Optional.</li>
          <li><strong>Details about your event</strong> — the date or rough timing, the type of event, and whatever you write in the message box.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Everything in that list is information you typed and chose to send. We collect nothing about you
          before that point, and nothing at all if you read the site and leave.
        </p>

        <h2>What we do not do</h2>
        <p>
          This is worth stating plainly, because most privacy notices describe practices this site does not
          have:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>We do not use cookies.</strong> This site sets none, which is why you were not asked to accept any.</li>
          <li><strong>We do not run analytics.</strong> There is no Google Analytics, no Meta pixel, and no other tracking script anywhere on this site.</li>
          <li><strong>We do not build profiles</strong>, run advertising, or track you from page to page.</li>
          <li><strong>We have never sold personal information</strong>, and we do not intend to. See the Nevada section below.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Who else is involved</h2>
        <p>Three companies necessarily see something when this site is used:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Web3Forms</strong> delivers the consultation form. When you press Send — and only then —
            your submission passes through their service on its way to our inbox. They process it to deliver
            it; they are not given it for their own purposes.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>GitHub</strong> hosts this site. Like any web host, their servers record the requests they
            serve, which includes your IP address. We do not have access to those logs.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Google Fonts</strong> serves the typefaces the site is set in. Loading a font from Google's
            servers means your browser contacts Google and, as with any request, your IP address is visible to
            them in doing so.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Those three are the whole list. We do not share your enquiry with anyone else — no lead brokers, no
          marketing lists, no other vendors.
        </p>

        <h2>Whether anyone tracks you across other websites</h2>
        <p>
          Nevada asks us to answer this directly. <strong>We do not allow third parties to collect information
          about your activity across different websites through this site.</strong> There is no advertising or
          analytics code here that could do so. Google's font service and our host see individual requests to
          this site; neither is used to follow you elsewhere.
        </p>

        <h2>How long we keep your enquiry</h2>
        <p>
          Your enquiry lands in our email inbox and stays there like any other message, so we can find the
          history if you book with us or come back later. If you would rather it did not, ask us to delete it
          and we will.
        </p>

        <h2>Reviewing, correcting or deleting your information</h2>
        <p>
          Email <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> and tell us what you want. You can ask
          us to show you what we hold about you, correct anything wrong, or delete it entirely. We may need to
          check you are the person who sent the enquiry — usually by replying from the same address you used.
          We aim to respond within a few business days, and there is no charge.
        </p>

        <h2>Nevada residents: the right to opt out of sale</h2>
        <p>
          Nevada law gives residents the right to tell a website operator not to sell their personal
          information. <strong>We do not sell personal information and never have</strong>, so there is nothing
          to opt out of. If you would like to submit a request anyway, or want that confirmed in writing, email
          <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> with "Nevada opt-out" in the subject line and
          we will confirm.
        </p>

        <h2>Children</h2>
        <p>
          We plan a good many children's parties, but we deal with the adults arranging them. This site is not
          directed at children and we do not knowingly collect information from anyone under 13. If you believe
          a child has sent us something through this form, tell us and we will delete it.
        </p>

        <h2>Changes to this notice</h2>
        <p>
          If we add anything to this site that collects or shares more than what is described above, we will
          update this page and change the effective date at the top before doing so.
        </p>

        <h2>Contact</h2>
        <p>
          Questions about any of this go to <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>, and a
          person reads them.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="legal-cta reveal">
        <p>Still want to get in touch about an event?</p>
        <CTAButton href="/contact">Book a Consultation</CTAButton>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
